take us all the way back to to the point  of which you are sat on the other side  of the desk  from where you are right now receiving  that diagnosis  so it was actually during my colonoscopy  that they push in the little bit of  sedation so  that you should relax and you lie back  and i glanced at the television screen  and saw something that was very very  clearly abnormal and  i turned around and looked at the  surgeon and  basically what's that and as his eyes  came up to make mine  i knew and the room got quieter and  quieter and everyone got kinder and  kinder and as they did  for me the worse it got because  essentially i had come to that  realization that it was  cancer on my own and just like that your  entire world  changes because while cancer tries to  take over your body it also tries to  take away  all the other things that you do be that  work or parenthood or  or friendship everything becomes about  cancer and it was really important to me  to be able to hold on  to something and for me that's always  been and i know it's a cliche to say  that you're a doctor because you want to  help people but for me that was  always sort a presiding aim  what what i puzzle over philippa is  whether or not  you you being a doctor made things  better or worse  obviously you're immediately able to  identify something as abnormal which  most of us would  would not be able to do but did your  knowledge of oncology did your knowledge  of bowel cancer  i mean did that make you feel even worse  than you presumably were or or did it at  least allow you  the ability to know what your doctor was  talking about in terms of treatment  plans  so there are pros and cons so i  understand medical ease i understand the  medical language  and i feel comforted when people use  that because that's my language so there  are pros  but there are real cons in that whilst i  always believe knowledge is power  doctors are taught  to think about the next steps what  happens if this doesn't work what  happens if that doesn't work and so  i've been in intensive care with things  going wrong and some part of my brain is  still  thinking in a very magical way someone  better come in and give me an infusion  of this drug or that drug and that  fights with the patient  who is just simply scared and so there  are pros and cons but i think that also  it's affected me how i am as a doctor  and that's because i realize now more  than ever  that not everything can be fixed and  that's okay so doctors always try to fix  even if it's just with ourselves come  back and see me next week  and actually what patients often need is  just somebody to sit  with them and say that's really tough  and i'm here  because we can do tough things it's just  it's easier to do than when we feel  supported and not alone  does that does that not perhaps make  your job even more difficult i know  i know a number of medics of my  acquaintance but they're largely in  hospital settings  and there is an almost invisible wall  sometimes  even pre-pandemic up between them and  the people that they treat the you know  they're in and out in and out and  all day long but but as a general  practitioner that that emotional  connection  with your patients perhaps is more  is more possible and certainly more  obvious  so i think that doctors are taught to  not give of themselves because if we  give of ourselves  always there will be nothing left for us  and so we do  have a wall but patients really  appreciate the human  and they really want to see that  sometimes and so it's about striking the  balance between  giving enough that patients feel  listened to  and feel that you understand them while  still protecting yourself and actually i  think that that's something that we need  to be teaching medical students much  more because the difficulties with the  wall versus the human  are essentially why one of the reasons  why we think so many doctors have mental  health problems because it  is such a difficult balance but patients  really do seem to appreciate  the fact that i truly understand now of  course it's still about them it's not  about me  but just knowing that somebody gets it  can make real difference  i'm philippa how are you doing at the  moment  so i had a hopping surgery in the  pandemic  in september and at the end of that i  was told thankfully that i  am cancer-free and that was one of the  real drives to publish the book  was that we have this image in our heads  of cancer being the beginning of the end  and 50  of us are going to get cancer at some  point but more and more of us are  surviving it and it seemed  really important to deliver that message  of hope but the truth is i have my first  set of scans and scopes at the end of  this month  and like any patient with cancer i'm  pretty terrified  yeah i can i can well i can't imagine  because i'm not in your position but  i i certainly sympathize with it if you  had a message though  to people in uh perhaps concerned about  aspects of their health  concerned about being a burden on people  like yourself  gps what would you say to them  the nhs is open i have the only job  where people  knock on my door come in and say i'm so  sorry to bother you doc my whole job  is for you to bother me the nhs is open  waiting will not help because when when  it comes to cancer time matters the  earlier you are diagnosed  better your prognosis is likely to be  two week breaks expected cancer clinics  are still open so if you have any  concerns about your health please please  contact your gp  the book is called doctors get cancer  too dr philippa kay thanks so much for  joining us in the program
 

